Chronic Lymphocytic Leukaemia (CLL)
Haematology & Oncology
Painless generalized lymphadenopathy, Unexplained fatigue/night sweats, Absolute lymphocytosis >5 x 10^9/L, Smudge cells on blood smear

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HMO-014
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of profound, drenching night sweats and rapid expansion of painful cervical lymphadenopathy requiring urgent evaluation for disease transformation or acute infection.
Location: Nursing home
Present at Visit: Patient and facility nursing staff
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage by the nursing facility reported an elderly patient with known stable CLL who developed sudden, severe drenching night sweats, rigors, and a rapidly expanding, painful neck mass overnight. Urgent in-hours assessment was required to definitively exclude acute severe sepsis or Richter's transformation, which carried a high risk of catastrophic decline prior to routine morning rounds.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I woke up completely soaked through the bedsheets, freezing cold, and this lump on my neck has doubled in size and hurts to touch."
History of Present Illness: The 78-year-old patient has a 5-year history of slowly progressive, previously asymptomatic Chronic Lymphocytic Leukaemia (CLL) managed with watchful waiting. They report a three-week insidious onset of worsening generalized severe fatigue and systemic lethargy. Last night, they experienced sudden, profound drenching night sweats requiring a full bed linen change. Concurrently, a previously painless, mobile cervical lymph node rapidly expanded over 24 hours, becoming severely painful and limiting neck rotation. They also note new early satiety over the past week.
Functional Status: Normally mobilizes with a frame; currently bedbound due to severe fatigue and rigors.
Social/Safety Concerns: Nursing home resident; frail baseline with compromised immunity.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 | HR: 112 | Temp: 38.6 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 95% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Frail elderly adult, visibly shivering in bed, wearing damp sleepwear.
Physical Exam:
Lymphatic: Massive, tender, asymmetrical right cervical lymphadenopathy (approx 6 cm) with overlying mild erythema. Palpable, firm, painless generalized lymphadenopathy noted in the left cervical, bilateral axillary, and inguinal regions.
Abdomen: Soft, but significant hepatosplenomegaly present. Splenic edge palpable 4 cm below the left costal margin, causing localized left upper quadrant discomfort.
Cardiovascular: Tachycardic, hyperdynamic circulation.
Skin: Marked pallor of the conjunctivae and oral mucosa. Scattered easy bruising on the forearms.
Environmental Observations: Soiled, sweat-drenched linens bagged in the corner of the room.
Medication Reconciliation: No recent changes; paracetamol administered by nursing staff 1 hour ago.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Chronic Lymphocytic Leukaemia (CLL) - Status: Exacerbated (Suspected Richter's Transformation or Acute Sepsis)
Differentials:

Acute Bacterial Lymphadenitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the massive, generalized nature of the existing lymphadenopathy and the presence of extreme systemic 'B symptoms' (night sweats/rigors) suggesting a malignant rather than purely localized infectious process.

Hodgkin Lymphoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the established primary diagnosis of CLL and the characteristic demographic and temporal presentation of disease progression.

Tuberculosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the rapid 24-hour expansion of the cervical mass and acute presentation of severe rigors, though the immunocompromised state requires a chest X-ray to formally exclude.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The sudden development of high fever, drenching night sweats, and rapid, painful expansion of a previously stable lymph node in a patient with CLL is a red flag for either a severe opportunistic infection (due to profound hypogammaglobulinaemia) or a high-grade Richter's transformation. The objective findings of tachycardia, fever, and massive splenomegaly indicate systemic instability. Delaying assessment until the facility's routine morning doctor visit would invite unmitigated septic shock or rapid malignant airway compromise. The urgent after-hours visit was absolutely critical to physically assess the airway, recognize the acute systemic decompensation, and coordinate immediate hospital transfer.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department via ambulance for urgent full blood count (anticipating profound absolute lymphocytosis and 'smudge cells'), blood cultures, and a lymph node biopsy to rule out high-grade transformation.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Allow the nursing staff to assist you with all mobility tonight. By avoiding independent movement while fatigued and tachycardic, you eliminate the high risk of a syncopal fall.

Report any new shivering or sweating episodes to the nurses immediately. By providing real-time feedback on your symptoms, you allow the medical team to accurately track the severity of the systemic inflammatory response.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Immediate ED transfer overrides routine GP follow-up.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ambulance transfer is required. Nursing staff to initiate emergency protocols if the patient's consciousness declines, if the neck swelling compromises breathing, or if hypotension worsens prior to paramedic arrival.

